// Practice quiz: INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/crud-aggregates-and-joins/insert-update-and-delete

::o1 two rows, named columns::Which statement adds two pets in one INSERT and names its columns? {
~INSERT INTO pets (pet_id, name) VALUES (1, 'Rex', 2, 'Mia');#Not quite. Four values for two columns is one malformed row, not two rows.
~INSERT INTO pets VALUES (1, 'Rex') VALUES (2, 'Mia');#Not quite. VALUES appears once, and this form also omits the column list.
=INSERT INTO pets (pet_id, name) VALUES (1, 'Rex'), (2, 'Mia');#Correct. One column list, then one parenthesized row per new row, separated by commas.
~INSERT pets (pet_id, name) (1, 'Rex'), (2, 'Mia');#Not quite. The statement needs INTO and VALUES.
}

::o1 why name the columns::Why name the column list in an INSERT? {
~It makes the INSERT run faster#Not quite. Naming columns is about correctness, not speed.
~PostgreSQL rejects any INSERT without one#Not quite. It is allowed but fragile, which is why the lesson names columns.
~It skips constraint checks#Not quite. Every constraint is still checked.
=Each value goes to the column you named, and columns you leave out get their default#Correct. The statement stays correct even if the table gains a column or its column order changes.
}

::o2 DELETE without WHERE::What does DELETE FROM pets; do? {
~Removes the last row added#Not quite. DELETE has no idea of a last row.
~Removes the pets table itself#Not quite. Dropping a table needs DROP TABLE; DELETE removes rows.
=Removes every row in pets#Correct. Without WHERE, DELETE targets every row in the table.
~Nothing until you add WHERE#Not quite. WHERE is optional, which is what makes this mistake easy.
}

::o2 preview first::Before running UPDATE pets SET owner_id \= 9 WHERE owner_id \= 4;, what is the safe preview? {
~SELECT * FROM pets;#Not quite. That shows every row, not the ones the WHERE matches.
=SELECT * FROM pets WHERE owner_id \= 4;#Correct. A SELECT with the same WHERE shows exactly the rows the UPDATE will change.
~SELECT * FROM pets WHERE owner_id \= 9;#Not quite. That shows rows that already have the new value.
~UPDATE pets SET owner_id \= 4;#Not quite. That is another change, not a preview, and it has no WHERE.
}

::o3 RETURNING after UPDATE::UPDATE pets SET name \= 'Max' WHERE pet_id \= 3 RETURNING pet_id, name; matches one row. What does RETURNING show? {
=That row with its new value\: 3 and 'Max'#Correct. RETURNING lists the rows the statement changed, with their values after the change.
~That row with its old name#Not quite. After an UPDATE, RETURNING shows the new values.
~The number 1 and nothing else#Not quite. The row count is a separate message; RETURNING returns rows.
~Every row in pets#Not quite. Only the changed rows are returned.
}

::o3 RETURNING after DELETE::What does DELETE FROM pets WHERE pet_id \= 5 RETURNING *; show when one row matches? {
=The removed row, with the values it had#Correct. RETURNING after DELETE shows each row that was removed.
~Nothing, because the row is gone#Not quite. RETURNING reports the row as it was deleted.
~The remaining rows#Not quite. Only the deleted rows are returned.
~An ERROR, because RETURNING is only for INSERT#Not quite. INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE all accept RETURNING.
}
